WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.515 --> 00:00:03.990
We have a very talented roster.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.340
We have a very talented team.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.930
But the reason we have
a chance to be great

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.580
is we're really good at grinding.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.450
I always think the best CEOs are grinders.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:13.950
Yes, you have to have a vision.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.600
Yes, you gotta set a culture.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.203
But the best ones, it's not
about them, it's the grinding.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.940
Grinding, it's a concept that resonates

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.100
in the blue collar town of Philadelphia,

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.830
a city that loves its football team.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.090
They'll even spell it out for you.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.913
E-A-G-L-E-S, Eagles!

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.600
I think Philadelphians are just eager

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.390
for any type of joy that we can get.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.483
Go Birds!

00:00:43.483 --> 00:00:47.190
I will say that our level of booing,

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.503
chef's kiss, best in the world.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.440
While Eagles fans are infamous,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.940
the team's owner, Jeffrey Lurie,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.400
may at first glance seem
like the polar opposite

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.080
to the Philly Faithful.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.930
A PhD wielding Hollywood film producer

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.030
who grew up in Boston?

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.493
That's not what many
have deemed a Philly guy.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:09.990
And in his time, he has made
some unpopular decisions.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:13.465
The Philadelphia Eagles
select Jalen Hurts.

00:01:13.465 --> 00:01:15.053
What?

00:01:15.053 --> 00:01:17.970
What the ?

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:22.140
We're not risk averse and we
never wanna be risk averse,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.970
whether it's on the football field or off.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.850
And that allows you to have this culture

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:30.090
that can achieve some
successes continuously.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.530
He's not your typical owner,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:35.700
and so he's not going to think or operate

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.170
like all the other owners.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.090
In his nearly 30 years with the team,

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.730
he's gone from being the
youngest owner in the league

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.640
to a standard bearer of
what modern NFL ownership

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:45.600
looks like.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.930
We're stewards of something
incredibly popular.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.320
Let's run it well but at the same time,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.120
let's really make a big difference

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.250
in the communities and across the country

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.313
and maximize our love for each other.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.870
Just off Broad Street
in South Philadelphia

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.150
sits the Eagles NovaCare Complex.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.180
I'm here today to meet with
their owner, Jeffrey Lurie.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.650
Now, if you're outside of Philadelphia,

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.600
you might not know his name

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.630
and that's not an
accident or an oversight.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:23.100
Lurie hasn't been one to sit down

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.690
for long interviews in the past,

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.510
but I wanted to understand
how he views himself,

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.760
his businesses, and the future of running

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.410
a pro football team.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.780
You know, as I was thinking about you

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.020
and preparing for this, it struck me,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.950
this is not a franchise
that has these massive highs

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.950
and then, like, stunning lows, as it were.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.200
There's a consistency to it.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.200
How do you create both a
culture and an organization

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.150
that is able to sustain?

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:57.900
So not easy, and I think what
we were able to do early on

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.663
is establish that culture.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:06.710
That culture is very intense, hardworking,

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:09.720
blue collar culture within an organization

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:14.190
that's very focused on the
key things that drive success.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.460
Jeffrey Lurie has owned
the Eagles since 1994.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.380
It's an investment that you'd have to say

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.310
has paid off for him.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.340
He bought the team for just
under 200 million dollars

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:28.200
and estimates today put the
team at 4.9 billion dollars.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.880
One of the things that I've found

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.680
to be true in pro football,

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.890
probably to a greater degree
than the average fan realizes,

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:39.890
is that ownership really
has an effect on the team.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.830
If you have a good owner then
you have a real chance to win.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:45.213
If you have a bad owner,

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:50.250
it just poisons the whole organization.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.980
NFL ownership styles
vary around the league

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.570
to say the least,

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.550
maybe best illustrated

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.380
by the owners within the
Eagle's own division.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:03:59.940
The New York Football
Giants have been owned

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.750
by the Mara family and their
offspring for nearly 100 years.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.780
It's a family business passed
down through generations.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.330
Down in DC, the newly
renamed Washington Commanders

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.493
are, for the moment, owned by Dan Snyder.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:15.510
Snyder is, to say the least, unpopular.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.410
Sell the team! Sell the team!

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.370
And his tenure has been plagued

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:23.460
by accusations of personal
and financial misconduct

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.050
leading to federal investigations

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.360
and calls even from fellow owners

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.153
to sell the team, which he may finally do.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.440
Then in Dallas, you have Jerry Jones.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.200
I hope there's some Cowboy fans here.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.820
Maybe the most recognizable

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.170
sports owner on the planet,

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:42.360
he has successfully marketed the Cowboys

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.540
into being the most valuable
sporting franchise ever.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.010
The team was valued by Sportico

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:50.880
at a staggering 7.6
billion dollars in 2022.

00:04:51.747 --> 00:04:53.250
There're all sorts of different owners.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.950
I mean, some owners like the visibility,

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:59.850
some owners get into this
because they love the visibility.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:03.480
After games, he stands in
the middle of the locker room

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.580
and people go to him before they go

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.910
to the coaches and players in
some cases, and he loves that.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.820
And Jeffrey is on the other side of that.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.340
If I'm tuning in to
sports radio, you know,

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:18.930
you're not doing a WIP call in show.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:21.750
I mean, this in and of
itself is pretty rare for you

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.570
to sit down and have an
extended conversation.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:27.750
Is that your personality?
Is it a business choice?

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.750
Like, why sort of take that approach?

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:34.290
So, you know, for me,
it's not wanting to...

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:37.590
It's much more wanting to
embrace the fans directly,

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:39.570
not through gatekeepers.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:44.570
So I always saw sports talk
radio as interesting, valuable

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:51.180
to, you know, for fans but
it's a very different business.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.390
We're in the business
of trying to win big,

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.250
have a great organization,

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.290
represent the the
community in a great way.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:02.280
We're not into wanting to monetize drama.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.260
That's in stark contrast to Jerry Jones,

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:06.150
whose strategy has meant repeatedly

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.850
placing and finding himself at the center

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:12.000
of every conversation and
controversy tied to his team,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.830
whether he means to or not.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:15.510
Financially, it's worked out

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.210
despite the team only
winning three playoff games

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.940
in the last 25 years.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.560
Lurie's far more reserved approach

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.040
puts him in a more familiar
setting, behind the camera.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:28.290
And local residents,
they're more than happy

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:29.850
for the spotlight.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.580
I think the fans are the star of the show.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:35.340
My name is Ahmir Questlove Thompson.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:39.990
I am a musician, member
of The Roots, a director,

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.000
and a proud Philadelphian.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.610
Quick time out here, Questlove?

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.050
We're gonna get more into this later.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:47.850
But beyond being a Philadelphia native,

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:50.250
six time Grammy award-winning musician,

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:53.970
author, and podcaster,
he's also a film director.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.670
He won his first Oscar
for his first movie,

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:58.560
a film called "Summer of Soul"

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.300
which Jeffrey Lurie executive produced

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.570
in his other business,
documentary filmmaking.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.063
But for now, let's get
back to those Eagles fans.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.460
Even though it's a cosmopolitan city,

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:13.263
it's really a blue collar town.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.600
It's a wolf in sheep's clothing.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.600
It's a blue collar town in the
drape of a cosmopolitan city.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.150
We're elegant, but we also
might have a pack of batteries

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:25.623
inside the inside pockets.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:30.570
Winning a Super Bowl
meant the world to me.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:33.030
And the first thing I did
after we won the Super Bowl

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:35.130
was get this Eagles lip tattoo.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:36.720
Whether it's lip tattoos

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.320
with the score of Super
Bowl LII, getting arrested.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:41.160
Go Eagles.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.593
Or whatever this is.

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:48.600
It's a passionate fan base

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.360
that is gonna let you know how they feel,

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.490
even while rescuing children from fires.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.920
Oh my man, just start throwing
babies out the window,

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.960
we was catching them, unlike Agholor.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:07:59.370
Local news stars aside,

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:00.630
with such a vocal fan base,

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:03.720
Lurie has often gone
against popular wisdom.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.570
With the second pick,
the Philadelphia Eagles

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:10.413
select Donovan McNabb,
quarterback, Syracuse University.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:13.680
And while some decisions,

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.600
like taking quarterback Donovan McNabb

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.970
over running back Ricky
Williams, worked out,

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:19.710
others not so much.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:23.310
The Philadelphia Eagles
select Jalen Reagor.

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:25.560
I've never wanted to
make popular decisions.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:28.890
I kind of grew up in Boston
with a baseball team at the time

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:30.870
that always kind of made popular decisions

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:34.470
and they always kind of
ended up not doing very well.

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:36.390
And I always thought, you know,

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.480
if I ever had the chance
to run or own a team,

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.150
don't ever try to make popular decisions.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:44.190
Try to make what you think is best.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:47.340
If we're gonna have to change a coach,

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:50.430
it's gonna be change for a great reason,

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:54.930
for a coach that can provide
something that's been missing.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:59.160
If it's a player mistake, don't continue

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:03.660
to make that investment in that
player mistake, but move on.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.530
Have sort of a self-critical nature,

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:09.090
that's part of the culture.

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:11.160
I think that's been one
of our keys to success

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.960
is we pay very little
attention to the outside noise

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.380
and recognize some of it is
true, some of it's not true.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:22.920
And the best thing you can do
is do what you think is right.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:24.570
And recently for NFL owners,

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:27.483
doing the right thing goes
beyond the football field.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:33.870
NFL team facilities tend to be shrines

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.900
to the players and personalities
of the team's past.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:37.950
But right as you enter,

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:39.480
you'll notice something a little different

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.250
about the NovaCare complex.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.800
A wall of heroes honoring
Martin Luther King Jr.,

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.830
Nelson Mandela, and Dr. Paul Farmer.

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:51.390
This is not the typical
entrance to a football facility.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.700
Very early on after owning the team,

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:58.110
I thought, you know, the
values we really wanna present

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:01.710
to our players, our employees,
everybody involved in it

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:03.720
is football is incredible,

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.940
it's the most popular sport in America,

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.430
but let the values be even bigger.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.610
We're out there, we're out
there to have a great practice.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:14.190
You know, we're singularly
focused on the next play,

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.680
the next practice, the next game.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.260
And yet our lives, we're human beings,

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:22.830
and this is a chance to kind
of put it all in perspective.

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:26.730
And it doesn't take away
from being hyper-competitive.

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:28.350
It makes you wanna make a difference

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:30.450
whether it's on the football field or off.

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:33.690
I think it ups your odds on winning.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:34.830
While over Lurie's tenure,

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:36.960
the Eagles have taken
up a number of causes

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:39.030
all around the Philadelphia area.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.730
More recently, Lurie's focused
his philanthropic efforts

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:45.090
closer to home on autism research.

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:47.010
Thank you all for being here today

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:49.350
and for your unwavering commitment

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:53.100
to helping improve the lives
of those impacted by autism.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:55.650
You know, I hesitated for a long time

00:10:55.650 --> 00:11:00.360
integrating my wanting
to try to make an impact

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.890
in the world of autism
'cause of my brother

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:06.690
and the family business, so to speak.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.240
It became something where I wasn't needing

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:12.630
to, like, push it aside,
but saying, you know what?

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:16.470
We could be a sports team
that absolutely identifies

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:18.810
with a underfunded condition

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:21.930
that affects millions around the world

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:26.930
and kind of make it so that
there's no real difference

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.510
between the Eagles and autism.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:33.960
As you notice, at the
end of every end zone,

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:35.850
we have Eagles Autism Foundation.

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:39.540
So every time there's a TV
shot of a score, a field goal,

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:43.380
whatever's going on, the Eagles
and autism are synonymous.

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:46.020
That was my goal because that allows us

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:49.050
to reach millions of people.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:52.950
It allows us to have terrific fundraising.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:56.490
It's amazing to see our
players and everybody

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:57.780
wanna be part of it.

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:00.780
Philanthropy across the
entire NFL is also changing,

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:03.090
spurred by a workforce, the players,

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.913
who have louder voices
and higher expectations.

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:10.740
Today's NFL occupies a
unique and important place

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:14.340
in American culture, reflecting
often, in stark terms,

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:16.650
the biggest opportunities and challenges.

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:18.630
Great wealth and individual success,

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:20.550
right alongside a history that includes

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:24.360
systematic racism, sexism,
and abuse of power.

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:27.030
The past several years have
seen NFL players fight for

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:29.280
voting rights, equality, and more

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:31.803
by leveraging their fame and influence.

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:37.150
Do you feel the expectations
have been raised for ownership

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:39.750
during your tenure as an owner?

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.640
I do, I do, in a good way.

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:45.120
I think we're really
challenged to be good,

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:47.100
really good community examples.

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.200
And let's not just be,

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.950
we're the NFL, we're so
successful, we dominate television,

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:55.230
our valuations are gigantic.

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:59.460
Let's absolutely be the
best citizens we can be

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:02.790
and let's judge ourselves
not just on the field

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:03.903
but off the field.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:05.940
Lurie's also leaned into

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:08.910
the interest of his players
when it comes to social issues,

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:11.970
most notably in the recent
past with Malcolm Jenkins.

00:13:11.970 --> 00:13:13.410
Are you not gonna say anything today

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:15.903
or just gonna use these posters?

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:21.120
I cherish having, you
know, players like Malcolm.

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:24.480
I encourage players to be
really community involved.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:26.550
I always really enjoyed his ability

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.760
to think through what
he wanted to accomplish

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:33.540
and then set a series of short
term mechanisms to do it.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:38.540
We are here to use our
leverage, our voices

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:42.780
to make sure that our families,
our communities, our kids

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:44.550
are a priority.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:47.220
Continuing this legacy of using the NFL

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:50.160
as a platform for change,
quarterback Jalen Hurts

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:52.860
has been raising awareness
around gun violence,

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:56.340
all with the support and
amplification of the Eagles.

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:59.610
I think the more important
thing is the platform we have

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:03.213
and the opportunity we have to
impact the people around us.

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:06.930
There's definitely an
intersection between sports,

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:09.480
entertainment, and popular culture.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:13.440
It's up to us, the leaders
in those industries,

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:16.410
to be able to celebrate
the beauty that we have

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:18.540
and attack the problems that we have.

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:23.130
'Cause there's real problems
of poverty, inequality,

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:25.170
social injustice, these are real.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:26.490
Yet at the same time,

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:30.690
let's celebrate the wonderful
people that we have.

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:33.600
Lurie's own off-field
platform, making movies,

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:34.980
allows him another avenue

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:37.260
to advance important conversations.

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:38.730
His films document seminal

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:41.700
but often little known
moments in American history,

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:42.810
stories that give voice

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:45.330
to our collective complicated relationship

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:46.773
with race and equality.

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:53.570
And the winner is "Summer of Soul,

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:56.157
When the Revolution
Could Not Be Televised."

00:14:57.506 --> 00:14:59.080
Ahmir Thompson

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:03.093
and four white guys.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:06.900
So here's a funny thing.

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:10.800
So the normal protocol,
especially with documentaries,

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.260
is you usually just have,
like, one benefactor,

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:16.890
like one producer, one
person to finance it.

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:19.560
This film was very
unusual in the fact that

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:23.190
everyone sort of wanted in on the action.

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:26.790
And when I saw the name Jeff Lurie,

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:31.290
I was like, "Nah, it's not him, anyway."

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.540
And went on and I saw the name again

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:34.373
and I was like,

00:15:34.373 --> 00:15:38.250
"Wait, isn't this the guy
that owns the Eagles?"

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:39.500
And they're like, "Yeah."

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:42.030
I was like, "Wait, he's
in the movie business?

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:42.863
Who knew?"

00:15:42.863 --> 00:15:45.903
Like I had zero clue.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:51.750
The movie industry was the family business

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:52.950
for Jeffrey Lurie.

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:55.350
His grandfather Phillip
Smith was an entrepreneur

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:57.270
in the movie theater business.

00:15:57.270 --> 00:15:59.070
He helped popularize the drive-in

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:01.710
and the suburban shopping mall theater.

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:04.740
It's interesting when you
parallel and look at, you know,

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:05.573
what I'm doing now,

00:16:05.573 --> 00:16:10.410
but it was all focused on making
the enjoyment of watching,

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:14.340
like the fan of movies,
enjoy their experience.

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:19.200
So big beautiful screens,
clean bathrooms, easy parking.

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:21.300
This was his way in

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:24.390
to try to get people to
appreciate storytelling.

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:25.260
Lurie has produced

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:26.730
some narrative films over the years

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:28.500
but of late, documentary has been

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:32.040
the effort of his company
Play/Action Pictures.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:35.460
Lurie received his third
Oscar for "Summer of Soul."

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:38.010
It got presented to us
as, "This is a project.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:41.130
would you like to help
from the very beginning

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:42.510
with Questlove?

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:46.560
And we've got access to the
archives of all this music,"

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:50.370
and we just thought it was
a perfect project for us.

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:54.450
And often we will take
stories that happen,

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:55.530
history that happened

00:16:55.530 --> 00:17:00.420
that has a very, very real
present day relevance.

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:03.690
Like I've seen rubber
stamping in the industry,

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:06.090
especially with the executive producers.

00:17:06.090 --> 00:17:08.250
You know, talk to my
people, they'll explain

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:11.850
and I'll do the final
signature, yep, it's good to go.

00:17:11.850 --> 00:17:15.630
Jeff is really, really
passionate about movies

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.800
and, you know, he had notes

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:22.350
for every rough draft that we sent.

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:26.550
You know, we did at least
five cuts of "Summer of Soul."

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.982
His notes were front and center

00:17:28.982 --> 00:17:31.056
Welcome to the Harlem Cultural Festival.

00:17:31.056 --> 00:17:32.220
Built around never scene footage

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.987
of the 1969 Harlem Cultural Festival,

00:17:34.987 --> 00:17:36.810
"Summer of Soul" ultimately tells a story

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:39.270
not just of an amazing lineup of artists,

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:40.800
but of what their hidden history

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:43.620
says about America then and now.

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:47.520
It started out just as a
cool mixtape of performances

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:52.520
and then it wound up
really being a life lesson.

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:56.283
It was one where I love
showing it to our players.

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:59.790
They were, in most cases,

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:04.790
like, shocked at the late
sixties and what was going on

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:08.430
both in the country
and the humanity of it.

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:13.430
So a lot of the parallels
here of owning a team,

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:15.810
having a documentary film company

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:18.690
is to explore the humanity

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:22.350
and elevate the value of human beings

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:25.230
that really need to be elevated

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:28.590
as an example in society for all of us.

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:33.590
And it strikes me that that
outlook, philosophy, ethos,

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.480
however you want to describe it, you know,

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:39.063
it comes into sharp relief
in the summer of 2020.

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:41.850
Protests over the death of George Floyd

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:44.430
while in police custody
now in their eighth night.

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:46.350
The remarkable scenes
that have been unfolding

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:49.290
in cities across this country yet again.

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:52.260
There's a lot of your work that starts

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:55.680
to come to a very fine
point at that moment.

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:58.500
Your worlds are colliding to some extent,

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:01.410
in terms of what you've
done as a filmmaker

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:04.350
and what you're doing as
the owner of this franchise.

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:06.090
Take me back to that summer.

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:11.043
So obviously very difficult
summer, but not atypical.

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:16.470
There's a lot of taking
down of Black citizens

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:19.500
all across the country
every day, every week.

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:24.090
But, you know, a terribly
polarized time in America

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:27.300
and a lot of anger coming out.

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:29.940
Suddenly the very issues

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:34.260
that we were dealing with in 1969,

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:37.350
we were also dealing with
those same exact issues

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:38.700
50 years later.

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:41.940
I will say that this
entire decade from 2020 on,

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:45.000
will be sort of a new establishment

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:50.000
and a new beginning for the platform

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:52.830
and who gets to speak on that platform.

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:55.560
While Lurie continues to work with

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:58.920
players and filmmakers in
areas of representation,

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:00.870
on the football field,
the Eagles have also

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:03.360
played a key role in
elevating Black players

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:05.700
to the most coveted role in the game,

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:07.113
the quarterback position.

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:09.870
Entering the 2022 season,

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:12.660
the Eagles had the most games
started by a black quarterback

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:16.230
in the history of the NFL with 360.

00:20:16.230 --> 00:20:18.060
In comparison, their rival,

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:21.600
the New York Giants had only one ever.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:22.433
You know, first of all,

00:20:22.433 --> 00:20:25.200
we're trying to get the
best possible quarterback

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:27.007
so it's not in any way,

00:20:27.007 --> 00:20:31.410
"Well, oh, okay, this person
is black," or whatever,

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:33.150
that's never been the case.

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:34.980
We always want to get the best player.

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:38.460
On the other hand,
whatever I'm involved in,

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:42.690
I think there's a feeling
of sort of undervalued,

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:46.080
certainly felt that with Donovan McNabb.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:50.370
Nobody had ever drafted a
black quarterback that high

00:20:50.370 --> 00:20:53.100
in the history of the NFL
at the time, number two.

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:54.720
We were lucky to get him, I mean,

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:57.960
we were so lucky that the
team who had the first pick

00:20:57.960 --> 00:20:58.920
undervalued him.

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:03.270
His mobility and his ability
to be dynamic on the field

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:06.600
were probably undervalued by
that team and goes on and on.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:08.370
With Michael Vick, it was more,

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:10.230
this man deserves a second chance.

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:13.350
The things he got involved
in were awful, you know?

00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:17.610
I describe him as heinous
with dogs and I'm a dog lover.

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:22.610
But we felt in America at
that time and still to today,

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:26.610
often black people don't
get second chances.

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:28.620
And this was a man who was very talented.

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:30.810
That's why he was getting a second chance.

00:21:30.810 --> 00:21:34.980
But also because we believed his desire

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:37.230
to make good on some of the, you know,

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:40.710
things that he caused a
lot of pain were real.

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:43.350
And Michael proved us to be right

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:45.240
for giving him the second chance.

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:49.020
So there's a history
to Randall, to Donovan.

00:21:49.020 --> 00:21:52.113
We had Rodney Pete, I could
add Jalen Hurts to that.

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:57.570
Just all excellent
people, comes down to it.

00:21:57.570 --> 00:22:00.450
Excellent people who
probably were undervalued.

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:03.150
And so whether it's Black
musicians in the sixties

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:05.100
or quarterbacks in the, you know,

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:08.883
nineties and two thousands,
there's a similarity.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:19.440
So I feel like we've been
like leading up to a moment

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:22.560
where I ask you sort of one of
these big sweeping questions.

00:22:22.560 --> 00:22:26.790
What's your advice, as the
future of the NFL unfolds,

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:28.893
for someone who is coming into this now?

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:34.080
Well first of all, come into
it if you love the sport,

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:37.560
don't come into it like you're
just running a business.

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:38.970
It's not the same.

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:43.470
Number two, manage your team
to the best of your ability

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:46.380
and not what outside world influences.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:48.570
Those are short term influences.

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:51.240
Don't pay attention to that stuff.

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:52.806
Don't try to be popular.

00:22:52.806 --> 00:22:56.610
That'll create unpopularity,
basically, in the long run.

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:57.990
Lurie's giving that kind of advice

00:22:57.990 --> 00:23:00.300
in real time to his son Julian,

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:01.830
who has worked for the NFL

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:03.990
and recently joined the Eagles full-time

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:07.170
in a business and football
operation strategy role.

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:10.380
Lurie Senior, having grown up
in another family business,

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:12.510
has turned the Eagles into his own

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:14.370
and plans to pass it down,

00:23:14.370 --> 00:23:16.320
with an expectation that his children

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:18.120
will run it with the same attention.

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:19.980
But it feels like 30, 40 years ago,

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:21.720
you could sort of be a
gentleman owner, right?

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:23.210
You could just sort of, like, rock up

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:25.080
to the stadium on Sundays.
I'm sure you could.

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:26.190
But today? No way.

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:27.750
No, and you won't be successful.

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:29.340
You gotta be really passionate

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:34.340
and you feel that weekly
ultra-competitive nature.

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:36.510
And it's also what makes it great.

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:38.430
I love that competitive juices.

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:41.250
You're not manufacturing widgets,

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:44.940
you are trying to maximize human beings.

00:23:44.940 --> 00:23:46.320
Even better than the movies?

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:48.300
Even better than the movies.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:50.700
Even much better than the movies, yeah.

